WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.512
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:10.782 --> 00:12.717
People have histories.

3
00:15.954 --> 00:17.622
Happy memories.

4
00:21.159 --> 00:23.061
Memories they would rather not recall.

5
00:34.072 --> 00:35.073
ARMY HAJIME KATAGIRI

6
00:35.140 --> 00:36.674
Lives that were buried.

7
00:41.179 --> 00:43.348
Lives that were inherited.

8
00:44.849 --> 00:48.386
The lives they lived have shaped this country.

9
00:50.288 --> 00:54.025
It's the Seika era, in the democratic country of Nipponia.

10
00:54.792 --> 00:57.795
A huge ruling party has held power for a long time.

11
00:59.097 --> 01:02.433
This once-rich country is now on the edge of a cliff.

12
01:02.967 --> 01:05.103
The gap between rich and poor keeps growing.

13
01:05.170 --> 01:07.305
Both blue-collar workers and yakuza have no money.

14
01:08.206 --> 01:14.846
In Nagatanicho, the center of this nation, a great thief has landed.

15
01:14.913 --> 01:17.348
Her surname is Kisaragi, her given name is Yayoi.

16
01:17.415 --> 01:20.718
She became secretary to Miyabi Kobayakawa, daughter of a political family.

17
01:20.785 --> 01:24.722
Her target is the secret funds in the Chief Cabinet Secretary's office!

18
01:25.223 --> 01:29.160
That's not all. She also steals any shady money she comes across!

19
01:29.227 --> 01:32.430
Who the hell stole that money?!

20
01:32.497 --> 01:35.300
The Kokuji Party's Financial Director, Masakado Natsume,

21
01:35.366 --> 01:38.469
set a trap to catch the great thief.

22
01:38.536 --> 01:41.206
If you like, shall we have dinner sometime?

23
01:41.272 --> 01:43.841
But he couldn't catch her tail.

24
01:43.908 --> 01:46.644
Your compassion is your weakness.

25
01:46.711 --> 01:47.745
I know.

26
01:47.812 --> 01:50.415
Inui, a mysterious information broker helping Yayoi,

27
01:50.481 --> 01:53.451
uses the account of a certain political group

28
01:53.518 --> 01:56.321
to steal money from a fundraising party.

29
01:56.387 --> 01:59.591
Also, "someone" will do their best so this account won't be investigated.

30
01:59.657 --> 02:00.858
Who?

31
02:00.925 --> 02:03.461
I'll donate ten million to the Prime Minister myself.

32
02:03.528 --> 02:06.297
Then, we won't owe him anything.

33
02:08.266 --> 02:10.935
What changed their lives?

34
02:13.071 --> 02:16.174
The history of this country's lost 30 years!

35
02:17.008 --> 02:18.343
PoliticaLupin!

36
02:25.483 --> 02:26.517
Who…

37
02:29.754 --> 02:31.222
Is Natsume here?

38
02:31.289 --> 02:32.123
Sir!

39
02:36.294 --> 02:37.495
What's wrong?

40
02:37.562 --> 02:38.763
Oh, calm down.

41
02:41.065 --> 02:42.133
It's about…

42
02:43.101 --> 02:44.369
that theft.

43
02:44.936 --> 02:47.005
-Yes? -Forget about it.

44
02:47.939 --> 02:50.141
-What? -No money was stolen.

45
02:52.277 --> 02:53.111
So,

46
02:53.811 --> 02:55.480
there's no need anymore

47
02:57.382 --> 02:58.449
to investigate it.

48
03:01.152 --> 03:03.554
-What? -We won't discuss that matter anymore.

49
03:03.621 --> 03:05.156
But, sir…

50
03:05.223 --> 03:08.192
Money was taken from the party fundraiser's transfers, wasn't it?

51
03:08.259 --> 03:11.496
The account the thieves used belonged to your late father's group,

52
03:11.562 --> 03:14.232
the 21st Century National Research Association, right?

53
03:14.299 --> 03:15.667
That account was stolen.

54
03:17.135 --> 03:19.570
It was stolen 25 years ago.

55
03:19.637 --> 03:20.738
So,

56
03:21.372 --> 03:23.207
that means someone used it.

57
03:23.274 --> 03:25.076
It has been stolen all this time?

58
03:25.877 --> 03:26.978
Did you file a report?

59
03:27.045 --> 03:28.780
-No. -Let's file one!

60
03:29.447 --> 03:30.915
We can still catch them now!

61
03:33.484 --> 03:35.019
-Kiuchi. -Yes.

62
03:35.086 --> 03:36.554
You are a good secretary.

63
03:37.121 --> 03:38.756
I trust you.

64
03:38.823 --> 03:40.625
Then tell me everything!

65
03:41.492 --> 03:44.495
They say, "Let sleeping dogs lie."

66
03:45.063 --> 03:46.664
That account…

67
03:46.731 --> 03:47.999
must remain stolen.

68
03:48.066 --> 03:49.567
That's what matters.

69
03:52.070 --> 03:53.938
From January, the ordinary Diet sessions begin.

70
03:54.505 --> 03:56.841
First, the Budget Committee.

71
03:56.908 --> 04:00.478
This is Rep. Miyabi's schedule during this period.

72
04:00.545 --> 04:03.748
Her mornings start early. At eight, a party division meeting.

73
04:03.815 --> 04:05.750
At nine, the General Affairs Committee.

74
04:05.817 --> 04:08.453
At noon, she has a regular meeting while having lunch.

75
04:08.519 --> 04:11.889
At 1 p.m., the plenary session. From 2 p.m., she listens to requests

76
04:11.956 --> 04:13.858
from local supporters and assembly members,

77
04:13.925 --> 04:17.161
and attends lectures from bureaucrats from the Ministry of Internal Affairs.

78
04:17.228 --> 04:20.331
At night, she attends meetings or social events with supporters.

79
04:20.398 --> 04:23.868
Miyabi's policy is not to accept corporate donations,

80
04:23.935 --> 04:25.603
but she still attends events to learn more.

81
04:27.505 --> 04:28.873
She arrives home at 11 p.m.

82
04:28.940 --> 04:31.909
She reads the next day's materials and goes to bed at 1 a.m.

83
04:32.510 --> 04:34.412
She works 14 hours a day, on average.

84
04:34.479 --> 04:37.982
The Labor Standards Act doesn't apply, so her overtime pay is zero.

85
04:38.049 --> 04:40.651
You have to be a real workaholic to keep up.

86
04:40.718 --> 04:43.821
From Tuesday to Thursday, this continues.

87
04:43.888 --> 04:46.791
Friday night, she goes to her district in Chiba,

88
04:46.858 --> 04:49.127
spends the weekend, and returns Monday.

89
04:49.193 --> 04:53.297
This repeats every week. Seriously, no joke…

90
04:53.364 --> 04:55.266
We're so insanely busy!

91
04:55.900 --> 04:57.668
Did I become a secretary for this?

92
04:57.735 --> 04:58.970
Why am I even doing this?

93
04:59.036 --> 05:00.204
Why am I here?

94
05:00.271 --> 05:01.406
I'm starting to forget!

95
05:01.472 --> 05:03.274
You can slack off. You're just the secretary.

96
05:03.341 --> 05:07.378
My pride as a pro won't allow me to do that.

97
05:07.445 --> 05:08.312
Oh, really.

98
05:09.013 --> 05:10.515
That thief…

99
05:11.249 --> 05:13.484
has been so quiet lately.

100
05:15.686 --> 05:19.056
Maybe it was really just a misunderstanding?

101
05:19.123 --> 05:20.391
Maybe the thief

102
05:20.458 --> 05:24.295
is too busy being swamped by Diet sessions to steal.

103
05:24.362 --> 05:26.364
The culprit is a Diet member?

104
05:26.431 --> 05:28.433
Or someone's secretary.

105
05:28.499 --> 05:29.667
I won't say who.

106
05:30.868 --> 05:32.203
Actually,

107
05:34.505 --> 05:38.543
I looked into the political group behind that account.

108
05:39.110 --> 05:41.813
It was Mr. Ichiro Kobayakawa's political group.

109
05:41.879 --> 05:43.481
Twenty-five years ago,

110
05:43.548 --> 05:46.350
the year the Ichiro Kobayakawa administration was born,

111
05:46.417 --> 05:48.085
that group dissolved.

112
05:48.152 --> 05:50.588
In those days, Nagatanicho…

113
05:51.889 --> 05:53.224
Let's stop.

114
05:53.291 --> 05:56.461
I don't want to become an old man longing for the past.

115
05:56.527 --> 05:58.996
You can go ahead. What is it? Were things better back then?

116
05:59.063 --> 06:00.031
No.

117
06:00.898 --> 06:03.768
It was rough and muddy. A terrible era.

118
06:04.469 --> 06:08.506
There was even more slush money back then, compared to now.

119
06:08.573 --> 06:09.941
Sounds terrible.

120
06:11.275 --> 06:13.077
But the debate back then…

121
06:14.979 --> 06:16.047
felt alive.

122
06:17.448 --> 06:21.085
Despite being busy, I looked into this.

123
06:22.153 --> 06:23.821
The account you used.

124
06:23.888 --> 06:26.324
It was Ichiro Kobayakawa's political group.

125
06:26.390 --> 06:27.992
But it dissolved 25 years ago.

126
06:28.059 --> 06:31.362
I also looked into this building.

127
06:31.429 --> 06:36.133
-Huh? -You bought this building 25 years ago.

128
06:36.200 --> 06:37.034
And also,

129
06:37.101 --> 06:40.037
there's the pocket watch in that drawer.

130
06:41.138 --> 06:43.074
Oh, I wasn't snooping around.

131
06:43.140 --> 06:47.411
I was looking for a wine opener when I stumbled upon it!

132
06:47.478 --> 06:48.779
Really?

133
06:49.714 --> 06:53.017
The name engraved on the back is Hajime Katagiri.

134
06:53.084 --> 06:56.754
This building's previous owner was also Hajime Katagiri.

135
06:56.821 --> 07:00.391
Who is he? I couldn't find him on the net or in party documents.

136
07:01.192 --> 07:03.361
So you're a detective now, instead of Lupin?

137
07:04.395 --> 07:06.764
Arsène Lupin was

138
07:06.831 --> 07:08.799
also a master detective!

139
07:08.866 --> 07:10.601
Wait, you didn't mean Lupin III?

140
07:10.668 --> 07:11.969
Tell me, please.

141
07:12.870 --> 07:15.439
I feel like I'm holding an unexploded bomb. It's unpleasant.

142
07:17.875 --> 07:19.043
Katagiri

143
07:19.877 --> 07:20.845
was a fixer.

144
07:21.412 --> 07:23.714
-A fixer? -Operating behind the scenes of politics.

145
07:23.781 --> 07:27.018
They coordinate interests, raise funds…

146
07:27.585 --> 07:28.886
Do the dirty work.

147
07:29.453 --> 07:31.422
Some left their names in history.

148
07:31.489 --> 07:33.558
Some ended up fading away.

149
07:53.511 --> 07:55.246
-Let me go! -Shut up!

150
07:55.313 --> 07:57.048
-Stay still. -Let go!

151
07:57.114 --> 07:58.215
Don't struggle!

152
07:58.783 --> 07:59.684
Hey!

153
07:59.750 --> 08:01.919
-Don't touch me… -Hold still!

154
08:01.986 --> 08:03.421
Don't you dare move!

155
08:09.193 --> 08:11.228
You trespassed on yakuza turf,

156
08:13.230 --> 08:15.099
and even fought back when caught…

157
08:16.767 --> 08:18.002
Do you have a death wish?

158
08:19.570 --> 08:20.638
What's your name?

159
08:27.545 --> 08:29.347
You must be hungry.

160
08:31.215 --> 08:32.950
-Excuse me! -Yes?

161
08:34.619 --> 08:37.321
The Kazamatsuri administration's approval ratings are falling.

162
08:37.388 --> 08:38.623
Will we have an election in the fall?

163
08:38.689 --> 08:40.124
You're so impatient.

164
08:41.092 --> 08:42.960
Our top priority now

165
08:43.027 --> 08:45.596
is passing the budget bill in March.

166
08:45.663 --> 08:46.897
The election can wait.

167
08:46.964 --> 08:49.300
Does that mean Prime Minister Kazamatsuri will stay?

168
08:51.268 --> 08:53.504
The Prime Minister is doing a good job.

169
08:53.571 --> 08:54.772
In the fall,

170
08:54.839 --> 08:59.377
I would like to introduce a colorful and refreshing personnel reshuffle

171
08:59.944 --> 09:01.946
to give Nipponia some excitement.

172
09:06.884 --> 09:08.386
Three years ago,

173
09:08.452 --> 09:11.689
I begged Mr. Shiro to make me Prime Minister.

174
09:11.756 --> 09:13.457
On the condition that

175
09:14.125 --> 09:16.227
he could handle the personnel appointments.

176
09:17.294 --> 09:21.132
The Chief Cabinet Secretary is Mr. Shiro's brother-in-law, you know!

177
09:21.198 --> 09:23.100
He was set there to monitor me.

178
09:23.167 --> 09:26.370
I was on foreign grounds, fighting a lonely fight.

179
09:27.338 --> 09:29.607
In my second term, I'll do what I like.

180
09:30.374 --> 09:31.275
Don't tell anyone.

181
09:31.342 --> 09:32.476
Of course.

182
09:34.311 --> 09:38.649
Outwardly, I can't get votes without Mr. Shiro's support.

183
09:39.617 --> 09:42.486
What is this "refreshing reshuffle" he talked about?

184
09:42.553 --> 09:43.954
About that…

185
09:46.123 --> 09:49.326
I want you to be the next Chief Cabinet Secretary.

186
09:50.494 --> 09:53.831
Mr. Shiro won't object if it's you.

187
09:54.699 --> 09:56.100
Good idea, isn't it?

188
09:57.068 --> 10:00.971
Even if the Oyama and Takamitsu factions have candidates, they can't win.

189
10:01.639 --> 10:05.776
Whoever has Mr. Shiro's backing will win. He's the biggest power.

190
10:05.843 --> 10:06.677
Yes.

191
10:07.878 --> 10:10.815
Prime Minister Kazamatsuri has made no big blunders.

192
10:11.549 --> 10:16.020
But he's just mediocre, and he loses 1% approval each month.

193
10:16.087 --> 10:18.422
In two years, it will be lower than the consumption tax rate.

194
10:18.489 --> 10:21.292
He can't win the next election like this.

195
10:23.327 --> 10:24.595
So, the question is…

196
10:25.229 --> 10:26.664
Who is next?

197
10:29.667 --> 10:30.568
Well,

198
10:31.769 --> 10:34.772
it would not be very refreshing if it were me,

199
10:34.839 --> 10:37.374
but there's no one else suitable.

200
10:38.075 --> 10:39.310
Natsume,

201
10:39.376 --> 10:42.446
won't you become Chief Cabinet Secretary

202
10:43.247 --> 10:45.182
to add color to my administration?

203
10:47.318 --> 10:48.152
That would be…

204
10:49.120 --> 10:51.255
an interesting reshuffle.

205
10:53.357 --> 10:56.227
What is this "refreshing personnel" about?

206
10:57.294 --> 10:58.596
We'll make Natsume

207
10:59.363 --> 11:00.364
Prime Minister.

208
11:03.667 --> 11:05.236
Just joking.

209
11:05.302 --> 11:08.038
He has no ministerial experience, so no one would agree.

210
11:11.108 --> 11:14.011
If Kazamatsuri doesn't do another term,

211
11:14.612 --> 11:15.679
we have Mr. Iwakura.

212
11:15.746 --> 11:18.048
He is a bit old though.

213
11:18.115 --> 11:20.417
Maybe Mr. Hasegawa.

214
11:20.484 --> 11:21.318
Yeah.

215
11:21.385 --> 11:23.554
Back then, politicians had stronger convictions

216
11:24.121 --> 11:26.657
and were truly substantial characters.

217
11:27.825 --> 11:30.795
Anyone could have become Prime Minister back then.

218
11:30.861 --> 11:33.164
I miss that era.

219
11:35.132 --> 11:36.033
It was you

220
11:37.601 --> 11:39.403
who crushed the substantial ones, no?

221
11:40.337 --> 11:41.338
You and…

222
11:42.473 --> 11:43.541
my father.

223
11:46.443 --> 11:49.613
Thanks to that, the Kokuji Party is invincible.

224
11:52.983 --> 11:57.321
Again, someone from the Kokuji Party liked every one of my posts.

225
11:57.388 --> 12:00.791
He liked my post about ending corporate donations too.

226
12:01.458 --> 12:05.896
Even if they don't say it openly, maybe some people think this is wrong.

227
12:07.164 --> 12:09.500
I hope such a person becomes Prime Minister.

228
12:09.567 --> 12:12.736
Who will you vote for in the leadership race?

229
12:12.803 --> 12:14.872
We don't even know who's running yet.

230
12:15.472 --> 12:18.709
Did you hear the rumor about the next Chief Cabinet Secretary?

231
12:18.776 --> 12:19.777
Who?

232
12:19.844 --> 12:21.545
Mr. Masakado Natsume.

233
12:25.115 --> 12:26.584
He might be a good choice.

234
12:26.650 --> 12:28.752
Right? He's a bit young though.

235
12:28.819 --> 12:29.820
That's his strength!

236
12:29.887 --> 12:31.922
That's impressive.

237
12:31.989 --> 12:33.457
NIPPONIA NATIONAL DIET LIBRARY

238
12:43.767 --> 12:47.071
KOBAYAKAWA BECOMES NEW LEADER OF KOKUJI PARTY

239
12:47.137 --> 12:48.472
ICHIRO KOBAYAKAWA

240
12:50.107 --> 12:52.676
Natsume, what about our promise?

241
12:52.743 --> 12:53.944
Promise?

242
12:54.011 --> 12:56.547
You said we should have dinner sometime!

243
12:58.482 --> 12:59.817
Do you not want to anymore?

244
12:59.884 --> 13:01.285
That's not true.

245
13:03.854 --> 13:05.256
How did you know this place?

246
13:05.322 --> 13:08.826
Kobayakawa versus Igawa, "Kawa VS Gawa." I know a lot about this.

247
13:09.693 --> 13:11.128
-Why? -It was my university thesis.

248
13:11.195 --> 13:12.062
Which university?

249
13:12.129 --> 13:14.632
Nothing big compared to your Tokyo University.

250
13:14.698 --> 13:16.100
You're free next Saturday, right?

251
13:16.166 --> 13:19.003
If you make it in time for Sunday's cherry blossom festival in Tochigi.

252
13:19.069 --> 13:21.572
-How do you know that? -Add me. This is my QR.

253
13:21.639 --> 13:22.673
Oh, okay.

254
13:27.077 --> 13:28.646
I'm back.

255
13:29.213 --> 13:30.247
Heard you got a date?

256
13:30.314 --> 13:32.416
I knew it was you.

257
13:35.653 --> 13:36.820
Why a daytime park?

258
13:36.887 --> 13:38.589
I don't know.

259
13:38.656 --> 13:40.457
-Is she wary of you? -Why would she?

260
13:40.524 --> 13:41.926
Will you ride a boat together?

261
13:41.992 --> 13:45.195
We'll just have lunch. Why are you in on this too, Honma?

262
13:53.137 --> 13:56.540
SNACK BAR ZEN

263
13:56.607 --> 13:57.708
Here?

264
13:58.609 --> 13:59.443
Yes.

265
14:03.113 --> 14:03.948
YAKITORI

266
14:04.014 --> 14:05.215
Hello.

267
14:05.883 --> 14:06.817
Hello.

268
14:13.157 --> 14:14.391
This is a chicken skewer place,

269
14:14.458 --> 14:16.560
but they have pork intestines too, called shiro.

270
14:17.861 --> 14:18.696
You know it?

271
14:18.762 --> 14:20.130
Let's eat.

272
14:27.638 --> 14:30.607
Kisaragi, did you watch that coverage?

273
14:31.909 --> 14:33.844
No one would decide on shiro out of nowhere.

274
14:37.414 --> 14:39.550
MASAKADO NATSUME

275
14:39.616 --> 14:41.552
FAVORITE FOOD: SHIRO

276
14:41.618 --> 14:43.554
WHAT DOES "SHIRO" MEAN? WHITE RICE?

277
14:44.254 --> 14:45.856
I saw an Upper House livestream on TV.

278
14:45.923 --> 14:47.491
You remembered well.

279
14:47.558 --> 14:49.927
It's rare for someone to write shiro as their favorite,

280
14:49.994 --> 14:51.295
so it stayed in my memory.

281
14:52.196 --> 14:53.330
I see…

282
14:59.236 --> 15:01.138
Let's eat.

283
15:07.011 --> 15:07.911
Hey.

284
15:09.913 --> 15:13.083
You were the one who suggested this, right?

285
15:15.319 --> 15:16.186
Yes.

286
15:16.253 --> 15:18.655
So could you stop acting bored?

287
15:21.792 --> 15:22.993
Sorry.

288
15:24.261 --> 15:26.764
Why do you like shiro? Shiro is poor people food!

289
15:26.830 --> 15:28.665
You're offending shiro fans.

290
15:28.732 --> 15:31.668
I thought maybe you liked to pretend to be a commoner.

291
15:31.735 --> 15:33.170
I guess "pretend" is too strong.

292
15:33.237 --> 15:37.241
You don't know my past. So treating me like this is an insult.

293
15:42.579 --> 15:43.447
I'm sorry.

294
15:48.552 --> 15:50.721
I used to like shiro too.

295
15:54.024 --> 15:55.993
You can chew on it forever,

296
15:56.060 --> 15:57.961
until the flavor is gone.

297
15:58.529 --> 16:00.130
Then I wouldn't feel hungry anymore.

298
16:00.931 --> 16:03.200
So if you just pretended to like it, I'd be mad.

299
16:03.934 --> 16:05.602
Sorry. I was prejudiced.

300
16:14.111 --> 16:18.849
That TV station suddenly sent me a survey, asking me to answer right away.

301
16:20.484 --> 16:21.752
Young people

302
16:22.853 --> 16:25.155
wouldn't even know what shiro is.

303
16:25.923 --> 16:27.958
I regretted writing it as my answer.

304
16:28.525 --> 16:29.593
When I was a child,

305
16:30.327 --> 16:33.097
in front of the supermarket where my mother worked,

306
16:33.163 --> 16:34.798
a chicken skewer stand was set up.

307
16:35.899 --> 16:37.734
Whenever I went to pick her up,

308
16:37.801 --> 16:39.636
she would buy me one skewer on the way home.

309
16:40.237 --> 16:41.772
I always ordered shiro.

310
16:42.272 --> 16:43.474
Because it lasted longer.

311
16:45.809 --> 16:47.111
That election

312
16:47.845 --> 16:50.881
was right after my mother died,

313
16:51.815 --> 16:54.551
so the question about my favorite food reminded me…

314
16:55.552 --> 16:56.954
I hadn't eaten shiro in years.

315
17:11.635 --> 17:15.239
As a kid, my mother and I lived in something like a shelter.

316
17:15.305 --> 17:18.142
I don't remember, but I think my dad often hit my mom.

317
17:19.743 --> 17:21.979
I often slipped out of that shelter

318
17:22.045 --> 17:23.847
to collect empty cans.

319
17:28.318 --> 17:31.755
A garbage bag full of cans got me 250 yen.

320
17:35.826 --> 17:39.096
On the way back, I bought shiro at a food stall.

321
17:39.830 --> 17:40.797
Fifty yen a skewer.

322
17:40.864 --> 17:42.199
I saved the rest of the money.

323
17:43.066 --> 17:45.636
But someone reported me.

324
17:46.603 --> 17:50.874
They thought some homeless people were making a child perform labor.

325
17:52.976 --> 17:55.712
It became quite a commotion,

326
17:56.446 --> 17:59.516
and we had to leave that place.

327
18:01.151 --> 18:02.252
To another shelter?

328
18:02.319 --> 18:05.989
Back then, people weren't really aware of domestic violence.

329
18:06.690 --> 18:09.393
There were also few shelters around.

330
18:09.459 --> 18:11.395
So we had nowhere to go.

331
18:12.196 --> 18:13.230
So,

332
18:13.864 --> 18:15.532
we lived in the mountains

333
18:16.300 --> 18:18.335
with others who also had nowhere to go.

334
18:25.409 --> 18:26.810
We had a house with a field.

335
18:28.011 --> 18:31.949
We planted seedlings and grew vegetables. It was quite organized.

336
18:32.015 --> 18:33.483
Welcome back.

337
18:33.550 --> 18:36.787
At its most populous, we had four adults,

338
18:36.853 --> 18:38.422
and like four or five children.

339
18:38.488 --> 18:40.424
Later, some grew up and left.

340
18:42.359 --> 18:44.361
I was in charge of cooking,

341
18:44.428 --> 18:48.665
since I knew how to cook for many people with cheap ingredients.

342
18:48.732 --> 18:52.970
The mothers there educated us and shared life experience.

343
18:53.537 --> 18:56.139
"Don't trouble other people."

344
18:56.807 --> 18:59.076
"Let's help each other."

345
18:59.142 --> 19:01.311
"Be kind even to strangers."

346
19:02.079 --> 19:02.980
Got it?

347
19:03.046 --> 19:04.381
Yes!

348
19:05.249 --> 19:06.783
But one day,

349
19:07.517 --> 19:09.152
we had to leave,

350
19:09.953 --> 19:12.055
and that life ended.

351
19:15.492 --> 19:16.760
It sounds like a lie, right?

352
19:18.228 --> 19:19.863
Because it is a lie. Don't worry.

353
19:21.765 --> 19:22.699
What about you?

354
19:22.766 --> 19:24.301
What kind of childhood did you have?

355
19:24.868 --> 19:25.736
You can lie, too.

356
19:30.941 --> 19:33.644
"You must not trouble others."

357
19:35.045 --> 19:37.047
I was told the same thing, growing up.

358
19:37.114 --> 19:40.183
Same as me. Was it the standard back then?

359
19:40.250 --> 19:41.351
Well, maybe even now.

360
19:41.418 --> 19:43.220
It isn't wrong,

361
19:44.054 --> 19:44.955
but I think

362
19:45.555 --> 19:48.992
you should teach kids to ask for help, too.

363
19:49.059 --> 19:50.494
Ask for help?

364
19:50.560 --> 19:51.395
Yes.

365
19:51.461 --> 19:54.831
A child raised without being allowed to depend on anyone

366
19:54.898 --> 19:57.601
matures early and loses the ability to ask for help.

367
19:58.735 --> 20:00.003
Because of that,

368
20:00.070 --> 20:01.838
my mother and I nearly died.

369
20:03.140 --> 20:04.074
Mom.

370
20:04.841 --> 20:05.842
Mom.

371
20:09.146 --> 20:10.180
I'm okay.

372
20:15.585 --> 20:20.157
I was always told not to trouble others.

373
20:20.991 --> 20:23.560
I thought if I nursed her well enough, she would get better.

374
20:24.394 --> 20:25.395
But my mother…

375
20:26.063 --> 20:28.098
just grew weaker and weaker.

376
20:34.171 --> 20:35.472
SHIZUO NATSUME - BIG BRO

377
20:43.880 --> 20:47.984
Our phone line was cut off because we couldn't pay the bills.

378
20:49.019 --> 20:50.120
Electricity and gas

379
20:51.421 --> 20:52.356
was cut off.

380
20:53.457 --> 20:55.125
So we lived on only water.

381
20:55.192 --> 20:56.126
Mom.

382
21:13.477 --> 21:18.682
After who knows how many days, in the evening,

383
21:18.749 --> 21:20.450
I suddenly woke up.

384
21:31.595 --> 21:33.230
I smelled dinner from a neighbor's house.

385
21:34.431 --> 21:37.067
It was curry rice. The smell drifted in.

386
21:41.338 --> 21:44.674
I had thought the world was just my mother and me,

387
21:48.011 --> 21:49.513
but there were other people.

388
21:51.982 --> 21:54.885
Other people lived in this world besides us.

389
22:00.891 --> 22:02.626
I decided to go outside.

390
22:06.730 --> 22:07.664
Anyone would do.

391
22:09.166 --> 22:11.401
I wanted to ask someone for help.

392
22:42.966 --> 22:45.535
That evening glow…

393
22:47.437 --> 22:49.473
I'll never forget it.

394
22:59.850 --> 23:01.518
-It's a lie. -It is?!

395
23:01.585 --> 23:02.652
No, it's true. Sorry.

396
23:04.287 --> 23:07.891
It's okay. After that, our life recovered.

397
23:07.958 --> 23:12.229
My sister lived separately from us. I only met her after I turned 20.

398
23:12.295 --> 23:13.430
So Ginji, my nephew,

399
23:13.497 --> 23:15.799
knows nothing about this.

400
23:18.235 --> 23:19.135
Huh?

401
23:19.703 --> 23:22.272
You're the first person to beat me at a poverty contest.

402
23:22.339 --> 23:23.406
It was a contest?

403
23:23.473 --> 23:25.542
There are fights you can't lose.

404
23:25.609 --> 23:28.078
I don't think comparing our misery would do us any good.

405
23:28.778 --> 23:29.813
If you want to fight,

406
23:32.349 --> 23:33.183
let's fight with this.

407
23:34.384 --> 23:35.252
I…

408
23:35.886 --> 23:37.754
have confidence I won't lose.

409
23:40.056 --> 23:41.224
Me too.

410
23:47.230 --> 23:49.165
WE'RE BATTLING TO SEE WHO CAN CHEW THE SHIRO LONGER

411
23:49.232 --> 23:51.434
Her bad habit is showing again.

412
23:51.501 --> 23:53.470
-What's wrong? -Oh, nothing.

413
23:53.537 --> 23:56.439
The appraisal result of your watch is in.

414
23:57.140 --> 23:58.008
Appraisal?

415
23:58.074 --> 24:00.410
Yayoi took a photo of it and asked me to have it appraised.

416
24:00.477 --> 24:03.580
It's a wartime army item. The name was engraved later.

417
24:03.647 --> 24:06.082
Without it, the buyback price would be 60,000 yen.

418
24:06.149 --> 24:07.150
You guys…

419
24:07.217 --> 24:09.185
Don't try to sell other people's belongings!

420
24:09.252 --> 24:12.055
No. Yayoi just wanted to know more about your past,

421
24:12.122 --> 24:14.457
but she said she had heard enough, so it's fine.

422
24:15.992 --> 24:17.527
It's not anything worth hiding.

423
24:17.594 --> 24:21.231
By now, it is just a story of a man nobody remembers.

424
24:27.203 --> 24:28.905
You're like a puppy.

425
24:29.940 --> 24:31.174
Do you want money?

426
24:33.243 --> 24:34.311
Of course.

427
24:34.377 --> 24:35.378
I see.

428
24:36.146 --> 24:40.183
But if you become a slave to money, you're done for.

429
24:41.151 --> 24:43.954
Money is important. If I had money, I would…

430
24:44.588 --> 24:46.022
What matters is

431
24:46.823 --> 24:48.692
what you do with money.

432
24:50.160 --> 24:51.094
I'll eat.

433
24:52.629 --> 24:53.897
Of course.

434
24:54.497 --> 24:55.765
Food is important.

435
24:57.067 --> 24:57.901
And?

436
24:57.968 --> 24:59.302
I'll buy a car.

437
25:00.036 --> 25:01.538
Too small.

438
25:01.605 --> 25:02.539
Buy a house.

439
25:02.606 --> 25:04.040
Still not enough.

440
25:04.107 --> 25:05.342
Then what will you use it for?

441
25:07.644 --> 25:09.546
Move the nation.

442
25:10.113 --> 25:11.381
With money…

443
25:12.048 --> 25:14.484
We can change this country.

444
25:14.551 --> 25:16.386
It's fun, you know.

445
25:19.823 --> 25:23.126
Katagiri was a man who rose in the postwar black market,

446
25:23.193 --> 25:24.995
who had strong ties with politicians.

447
25:25.061 --> 25:25.895
Boss.

448
25:26.563 --> 25:27.697
It's time.

449
25:33.103 --> 25:35.972
Oh, thank you for the other day.

450
25:37.073 --> 25:38.375
Watch.

451
25:38.441 --> 25:40.677
That man will become Prime Minister.

452
25:42.979 --> 25:44.314
Is he that great?

453
25:46.349 --> 25:48.451
I will make him Prime Minister.

454
25:51.921 --> 25:52.889
Sir.

455
25:52.956 --> 25:55.692
Shigeaki Igawa was in the left faction of the Kokuji Party.

456
25:55.759 --> 25:57.427
Mainstream conservative,

457
25:57.494 --> 25:59.629
favoring light military and prioritizing the economy.

458
26:00.163 --> 26:04.567
In those days, many Kokuji Party politicians spoke out against war.

459
26:05.702 --> 26:07.804
Those who survived the war

460
26:07.871 --> 26:10.874
were all cowards like me.

461
26:10.940 --> 26:14.077
The smart and kind ones all died.

462
26:14.144 --> 26:16.046
Wouldn't smart people survive?

463
26:16.546 --> 26:19.382
The smarter you are, the less you can stand it.

464
26:21.017 --> 26:22.485
You have to kill the enemy, right?

465
26:23.319 --> 26:25.221
Even if you can call them the enemy,

466
26:25.955 --> 26:28.958
their faces look just like your own.

467
26:30.060 --> 26:34.264
The dead bodies' eyes keep staring at you.

468
26:35.432 --> 26:37.734
You can't escape, no matter how much you try.

469
26:37.801 --> 26:39.536
They follow you even in sleep.

470
26:41.604 --> 26:43.740
We even killed our own comrades.

471
26:43.807 --> 26:45.375
It was called "disposal".

472
26:46.309 --> 26:48.445
We killed the sick.

473
26:49.345 --> 26:51.748
We killed over food.

474
26:53.183 --> 26:56.753
That sticky blood doesn't come off.

475
26:57.921 --> 26:59.589
It stank.

476
27:02.158 --> 27:05.695
We were covered in blood, maggots, and mud.

477
27:05.762 --> 27:08.465
"What am I even doing?" we thought.

478
27:08.531 --> 27:10.166
It was damn stupid.

479
27:21.444 --> 27:26.583
At the repatriation, my commanding officer tried to take this from me.

480
27:27.250 --> 27:28.284
"Don't be stupid."

481
27:28.351 --> 27:31.788
"You're no longer my commanding officer. This is mine."

482
27:31.855 --> 27:33.890
I never gave it up.

483
27:36.693 --> 27:37.994
I won't let anyone

484
27:38.995 --> 27:41.064
take anything from me again.

485
27:43.767 --> 27:44.968
Some time later,

486
27:45.535 --> 27:48.204
on Katagiri's orders, I became a Diet member's secretary.

487
27:48.271 --> 27:49.105
Sir.

488
27:49.172 --> 27:51.241
But I was not Igawa's secretary.

489
27:51.307 --> 27:53.710
I was planted as Ichiro Kobayakawa's secretary.

490
27:55.712 --> 27:57.313
This is my new secretary.

491
27:57.380 --> 27:58.314
At the time,

492
27:58.381 --> 28:02.185
Kobayakawa and Igawa were fighting fiercely inside the party.

493
28:02.886 --> 28:04.621
Face reality, please!

494
28:05.388 --> 28:08.057
We're surrounded by countries firing missiles!

495
28:08.124 --> 28:11.628
-A country without weapons is only prey. -Mr. Kobayakawa.

496
28:11.694 --> 28:13.563
Did you forget what happened just 50 years ago?

497
28:13.630 --> 28:15.732
Do you want to see the country turned to ashes again?

498
28:15.799 --> 28:17.767
Once a war starts,

499
28:17.834 --> 28:20.069
it continues until everything is destroyed.

500
28:20.136 --> 28:23.306
No war ever ends easily. That's rosy idealism.

501
28:23.373 --> 28:26.509
Peaceful diplomacy is the most realistic option!

502
28:26.576 --> 28:29.679
Protect our economy, the second largest in the world,

503
28:29.746 --> 28:32.081
and keep our position as an electronics nation.

504
28:32.782 --> 28:35.385
That is what will protect our country!

505
28:35.451 --> 28:36.953
Looking back,

506
28:37.954 --> 28:40.023
it was that debate which foretold

507
28:40.590 --> 28:42.292
the direction Nipponia would take.

508
28:42.358 --> 28:44.527
The winner of that leadership race

509
28:45.395 --> 28:46.830
was Ichiro Kobayakawa.

510
28:51.301 --> 28:52.602
You carry a book around?

511
28:52.669 --> 28:54.204
I was reading it on the bus.

512
28:55.338 --> 28:58.374
It's a book by a notable man, called a political "fixer."

513
28:58.441 --> 29:03.913
It even writes about billions in money influencing past leadership races.

514
29:03.980 --> 29:05.048
Likely a bit exaggerated.

515
29:05.114 --> 29:08.318
Ichiro Kobayakawa became party leader with the power of money.

516
29:08.384 --> 29:09.752
Just like other past leaders.

517
29:09.819 --> 29:11.287
Where did Ichiro's funding come from?

518
29:11.354 --> 29:12.255
-The CIA. -The CIA.

519
29:12.322 --> 29:14.290
-Yes. -It would be a huge scandal!

520
29:14.357 --> 29:16.659
A leader, receiving money from foreign interests.

521
29:16.726 --> 29:19.162
A clear violation of the political funds law.

522
29:19.229 --> 29:22.599
He would be called a spy. A traitor.

523
29:22.665 --> 29:26.336
His political career would be over if that came out.

524
29:26.402 --> 29:28.171
If it were true, that is.

525
29:28.705 --> 29:29.939
But there's no proof.

526
29:31.140 --> 29:33.276
A few days before the leadership race,

527
29:33.977 --> 29:35.745
Katagiri called me.

528
29:36.713 --> 29:38.348
"I got hold of a huge scandal."

529
29:38.982 --> 29:39.916
He said so.

530
29:39.983 --> 29:43.653
Proof that someone has sold off the nation's sovereignty.

531
29:43.720 --> 29:45.521
If this came out,

532
29:46.222 --> 29:48.658
Ichiro Kobayakawa would be a dead man.

533
29:52.996 --> 29:54.030
But then the dead man

534
29:54.831 --> 29:56.399
turned out to be Katagiri himself.

535
30:02.105 --> 30:04.807
The police treated it as a drunk-driving accident.

536
30:05.608 --> 30:07.010
The bag containing the proof

537
30:07.543 --> 30:09.279
was never found.

538
30:12.882 --> 30:17.787
Therefore, under Article 23 of the party leader election rules,

539
30:17.854 --> 30:22.625
Ichiro Kobayakawa is determined to be the winner.

540
30:22.692 --> 30:25.461
THE NEW LEADER IS ICHIRO KOBAYAKAWA!

541
30:25.528 --> 30:26.663
All right!

542
30:27.530 --> 30:28.865
Wow, well done!

543
30:38.441 --> 30:41.844
Mr. Katagiri lost his family in the Tokyo air raids

544
30:41.911 --> 30:43.346
and had no heirs.

545
30:46.683 --> 30:49.285
He had left a will saying that after his death,

546
30:50.286 --> 30:52.956
his assets would go to you, Mr. Inui.

547
31:06.903 --> 31:08.771
ARMY HAJIME KATAGIRI

548
31:09.939 --> 31:13.009
I won't let anyone take anything from me again.

549
31:24.420 --> 31:27.223
Nipponia needs structural reform…

550
31:27.290 --> 31:29.859
We have to give young people chances…

551
31:29.926 --> 31:31.861
It will make the labor market flexible…

552
31:31.928 --> 31:34.764
No, it will just give companies a blank check to fire people!

553
31:34.831 --> 31:36.299
TWO THOUSAND WORKERS FIRED

554
31:36.366 --> 31:38.368
A STAGGERING ECONOMIC DEBT BURDENS THE PEOPLE

555
31:38.434 --> 31:40.303
TO REINVIGORATE COLLAPSED BUSINESSES

556
31:40.370 --> 31:41.838
DOWN WITH THE INDUSTRIAL WASTE FACILITY!

557
31:41.904 --> 31:43.106
WE WILL NOT BE EVICTED

558
31:48.177 --> 31:51.581
A politician is coming to inspect us today.

559
31:51.647 --> 31:54.183
So be good girls, okay?

560
31:54.250 --> 31:55.218
Okay.

561
31:56.619 --> 31:58.121
Will we have to leave?

562
31:58.187 --> 31:59.555
It will be okay.

563
31:59.622 --> 32:01.391
-Just do your best. -Yeah.

564
32:03.793 --> 32:05.061
Nice to meet you.

565
32:05.128 --> 32:06.629
Hello. Please follow me.

566
32:07.430 --> 32:08.464
Please be careful.

567
32:11.334 --> 32:13.736
Please think about us!

568
32:15.438 --> 32:16.839
What will you do?

569
32:16.906 --> 32:19.108
Construction is already decided.

570
32:19.175 --> 32:21.411
Public works have decreased due to privatization,

571
32:21.477 --> 32:23.646
so we need a new outlet for vested interests.

572
32:23.713 --> 32:25.348
There's nothing I can do.

573
32:26.015 --> 32:28.051
If you want me to help,

574
32:28.117 --> 32:30.653
at least bring me donations.

575
32:31.587 --> 32:32.922
That's true.

576
32:33.656 --> 32:34.791
That's a tough request.

577
32:37.026 --> 32:39.395
If, 25 years ago,

578
32:40.063 --> 32:42.899
Igawa had won and become Prime Minister,

579
32:42.965 --> 32:44.267
what would have happened?

580
32:44.834 --> 32:47.236
Maybe not much would have changed.

581
32:48.237 --> 32:50.106
After the bubble burst, Nipponia…

582
32:50.807 --> 32:53.643
had to handle a massive amount of non-performing loans.

583
32:54.977 --> 32:57.213
But at that turning point,

584
32:57.880 --> 33:01.250
there was a major shift in direction toward policies favoring corporations,

585
33:01.317 --> 33:03.519
and that led to an unequal society.

586
33:04.120 --> 33:05.054
That's the truth.

587
33:06.389 --> 33:09.092
If the top gets rich, wealth trickles down.

588
33:09.158 --> 33:11.594
Trickle-down was just an armchair theory.

589
33:12.695 --> 33:17.100
In reality, companies retained earnings, and people's take-home pay never rose.

590
33:17.667 --> 33:19.602
The low birthrate isn't the only cause.

591
33:20.837 --> 33:22.405
Right now, this country is distorted.

592
33:22.472 --> 33:23.539
Now?

593
33:26.109 --> 33:28.978
Wasn't it distorted all along?

594
33:30.279 --> 33:31.848
Well, I don't care either way.

595
33:38.354 --> 33:41.624
You always talk that way.

596
33:43.259 --> 33:45.194
As if none of this concerns you.

597
33:46.129 --> 33:47.163
You push things away.

598
33:47.663 --> 33:48.664
Do I?

599
33:48.731 --> 33:50.867
It's like you have a grudge against politics.

600
33:54.704 --> 33:56.706
Politics can save people.

601
33:57.907 --> 33:59.742
After my mother and I nearly died,

602
34:00.309 --> 34:02.411
we benefitted from welfare.

603
34:03.713 --> 34:04.647
I studied,

604
34:05.581 --> 34:06.949
became a bureaucrat,

605
34:07.917 --> 34:09.952
and now shoulder this country's finances.

606
34:11.053 --> 34:12.021
By doing so,

607
34:12.088 --> 34:14.624
I can help people who were like me back then.

608
34:17.159 --> 34:19.128
Politics have saved me.

609
34:20.663 --> 34:21.864
That's why I'm here.

610
34:26.402 --> 34:27.737
Sorry.

611
34:29.972 --> 34:31.707
I'm just lecturing you now.

612
34:35.111 --> 34:36.546
I was also…

613
34:39.148 --> 34:40.316
I was also…

614
34:41.450 --> 34:43.186
saved by politics.

615
34:58.067 --> 34:59.635
Tear it down!

616
35:20.823 --> 35:22.625
Because I was saved by politics,

617
35:23.559 --> 35:24.961
I'm here now.

618
35:27.997 --> 35:28.898
We are…

619
35:29.498 --> 35:30.366
so similar.

620
35:47.149 --> 35:49.051
That was fun.

621
35:50.319 --> 35:51.754
Until halfway through.

622
35:59.595 --> 36:02.431
And what happened to Igawa Shigeaki?

623
36:02.498 --> 36:04.267
After he lost that leadership race.

624
36:04.333 --> 36:06.068
He was finished.

625
36:06.636 --> 36:09.205
He had received large bribes from general contractors.

626
36:09.272 --> 36:11.807
That was leaked, and he resigned as a Diet member.

627
36:11.874 --> 36:12.942
The end.

628
36:13.009 --> 36:15.578
What? Igawa also had slush money?

629
36:16.178 --> 36:19.582
Everyone is corrupt, man!

630
36:19.649 --> 36:21.751
So does that mean Mr. Katagiri died for nothing?

631
36:25.321 --> 36:27.723
Don't say it that lightly.

632
36:27.790 --> 36:30.459
And Ichiro Kobayakawa got away scot-free.

633
36:31.627 --> 36:33.162
Is there any proof he was murdered?

634
36:34.297 --> 36:35.898
There's a money transfer record.

635
36:36.499 --> 36:37.533
I found it.

636
36:39.001 --> 36:40.670
21ST CENTURY NATIONAL RESEARCH ASSOCIATION

637
36:45.975 --> 36:48.778
The date is the day before Katagiri died.

638
36:48.844 --> 36:53.649
The recipient is a company that had donated to Kobayakawa for years.

639
36:56.585 --> 36:57.620
Thirty million yen.

640
36:58.487 --> 37:00.690
Payment to the drunk-driving culprit.

641
37:00.756 --> 37:02.992
That company paid him to kill.

642
37:03.059 --> 37:04.493
Do you have proof they paid him?

643
37:05.161 --> 37:07.063
-Couldn't find any. -What?

644
37:07.129 --> 37:08.798
But Ichiro Kobayakawa

645
37:08.864 --> 37:12.034
never filed a theft report when this passbook disappeared.

646
37:12.101 --> 37:13.502
That's the answer.

647
37:14.103 --> 37:17.740
After that, I kept working, pretending to know nothing,

648
37:17.807 --> 37:20.476
and left two years later, on good terms.

649
37:22.278 --> 37:23.546
This is the Kobayakawas'…

650
37:24.313 --> 37:25.848
Pandora's box.

651
37:25.915 --> 37:27.983
The passbook was stolen?

652
37:28.050 --> 37:29.552
Only that passbook.

653
37:29.618 --> 37:33.589
-Why did you leave a paper trail? -I had no time.

654
37:33.656 --> 37:36.492
No, it's actually convenient.

655
37:37.793 --> 37:41.430
If the thief used that account record to accuse us,

656
37:41.497 --> 37:42.898
we'll say it was stolen.

657
37:43.666 --> 37:45.768
What about the funds report?

658
37:45.835 --> 37:47.436
File to dissolve our organization.

659
37:48.337 --> 37:49.572
But date it last month.

660
37:49.638 --> 37:50.573
Yes.

661
38:01.617 --> 38:03.753
I'm home.

662
38:03.819 --> 38:05.421
Representative Miyabi…

663
38:06.288 --> 38:07.423
Care to drink with me?

664
38:08.557 --> 38:09.592
No, thanks.

665
38:11.560 --> 38:12.395
Who…

666
38:13.829 --> 38:16.098
do you want to be Prime Minister?

667
38:17.633 --> 38:19.702
Someone who does politics for Nipponia's sake.

668
38:19.769 --> 38:20.970
That's only natural.

669
38:21.036 --> 38:21.937
Is it?

670
38:22.905 --> 38:25.040
Our family has devoted itself

671
38:25.641 --> 38:28.210
to this nation, for generations.

672
38:29.545 --> 38:30.946
What do you mean by "nation"?

673
38:32.181 --> 38:34.650
Do you know how English says it?

674
38:34.717 --> 38:36.085
"Country," right?

675
38:36.652 --> 38:38.454
"Country" is the land.

676
38:39.688 --> 38:44.260
"State" is the government. The government isn't the country.

677
38:44.326 --> 38:46.028
They also use the word "nation".

678
38:46.095 --> 38:49.064
The nation is a community of people and culture,

679
38:49.131 --> 38:50.499
in short, the citizens.

680
38:51.834 --> 38:55.004
The Japanese term "kuni" is vague, in comparison.

681
38:56.005 --> 38:58.274
The "nation" you care about…

682
38:58.908 --> 38:59.775
Is it the land?

683
39:00.409 --> 39:01.310
The government?

684
39:01.377 --> 39:02.678
Or the people?

685
39:06.816 --> 39:08.384
Here are my views.

686
39:09.251 --> 39:12.588
The nation exists to help its people.

687
39:13.355 --> 39:15.658
Not to serve the government.

688
39:15.724 --> 39:20.396
So I want a Prime Minister who does politics for the people.

689
39:20.463 --> 39:22.565
Don't talk nonsense.

690
39:24.733 --> 39:26.035
A Prime Minister

691
39:27.136 --> 39:28.537
is a portable shrine.

692
39:30.573 --> 39:34.410
Only when the people around one carry him, does a Prime Minister exist.

693
39:37.446 --> 39:38.948
The shrine itself…

694
39:42.017 --> 39:44.186
should be as light as possible.

695
39:51.560 --> 39:55.264
Is it my fault the approval ratings aren't rising?

696
39:55.831 --> 39:59.235
If I had been acting on my own judgment and decisions…

697
39:59.301 --> 40:02.104
You chose that yourself, Mr. Kazamatsuri.

698
40:02.171 --> 40:03.138
Now, now.

699
40:04.406 --> 40:06.242
He's right.

700
40:06.308 --> 40:08.077
I feel responsible too.

701
40:09.211 --> 40:11.680
What do you think, Ienaga?

702
40:11.747 --> 40:16.018
Perhaps we should leave it to the Prime Minister to decide.

703
40:16.785 --> 40:19.555
If he's confident in his judgment,

704
40:19.622 --> 40:20.756
then in the next term,

705
40:20.823 --> 40:24.159
he can surely raise approval and win the election,

706
40:24.793 --> 40:26.795
fair and square.

707
40:26.862 --> 40:31.367
If he's staking his political career on such a belief,

708
40:32.868 --> 40:36.572
I have nothing left to say.

709
40:36.639 --> 40:39.074
If you say approval is a matter of responsibility,

710
40:40.376 --> 40:43.512
who will take responsibility for these lost 30 years?

711
40:43.579 --> 40:46.382
Thirty years ago, there was a coalition government.

712
40:47.016 --> 40:49.752
But for the past 25 years,

713
40:50.586 --> 40:53.322
the Kokuji Party has held the helm.

714
40:53.389 --> 40:55.991
Who made this country what it is right now?

715
40:56.725 --> 40:58.861
That's a far worse sin than some approval ratings.

716
41:00.429 --> 41:04.233
You became Prime Minister with my backing, didn't you?

717
41:04.300 --> 41:05.768
I had no other choice.

718
41:08.704 --> 41:10.372
Admirable.

719
41:10.439 --> 41:12.274
But what you just said is unacceptable.

720
41:14.443 --> 41:15.311
Thank you…

721
41:16.178 --> 41:17.713
for the past three years.

722
41:23.652 --> 41:25.821
When do you want to announce that you won't be running?

723
41:25.888 --> 41:26.889
Iwakura.

724
41:28.123 --> 41:30.459
Do you really think you can become Prime Minister?

725
41:32.061 --> 41:35.064
"A refreshing reshuffle."

726
41:36.198 --> 41:37.900
Who do you want to be Prime Minister?

727
41:38.601 --> 41:40.235
I think it will be the same, anyway.

728
41:41.136 --> 41:44.206
Yayoi, you're surprisingly pessimistic.

729
41:44.873 --> 41:46.809
-Pe…? -Seeing the negative side?

730
41:46.875 --> 41:47.977
You're nihilistic.

731
41:48.744 --> 41:51.080
I do like dark poetry.

732
41:51.146 --> 41:52.114
What?!

733
41:54.416 --> 41:56.352
Kyoko Kabashima!

734
41:56.418 --> 41:59.521
I would like her to be Prime Minister.

735
41:59.588 --> 42:00.889
But she's already retired.

736
42:08.731 --> 42:12.768
-You're Miyabi Kobayakawa, right? -Yes.

737
42:12.835 --> 42:15.738
Don't become like your father.

738
42:16.705 --> 42:17.773
I won't.

739
42:18.540 --> 42:23.579
Excuse me, I saw your interview on TV, and was so impressed.

740
42:23.646 --> 42:24.847
That's why I'm here now.

741
42:24.913 --> 42:26.782
Wow…

742
42:27.683 --> 42:29.084
Which interview was that?

743
42:35.424 --> 42:36.525
That ring…

744
42:38.894 --> 42:40.295
It looks lovely.

745
42:41.096 --> 42:43.632
Oh, it's my birthstone.

746
42:43.699 --> 42:45.567
I bought it when I first won my seat.

747
42:46.402 --> 42:48.470
Well, see you.

748
42:48.537 --> 42:49.405
Yes.

749
42:53.942 --> 42:55.277
She's so cool.

750
43:04.186 --> 43:05.521
It really is all the same.

751
43:06.488 --> 43:08.957
No matter who becomes Prime Minister.

752
43:11.827 --> 43:13.362
GENERAL ELECTION SUPERVISING COMMITTEE

753
43:18.200 --> 43:22.271
Therefore, under Article 23 of the party leader election rules,

754
43:22.337 --> 43:25.541
Mr. Yoshimi Tamaya is determined to be the winner.

755
43:47.796 --> 43:52.334
NEXT KOKUJI PARTY LEADER YOSHIMI TAMAYA

756
43:53.402 --> 43:55.137
Wait, who is that guy?!

757
43:55.204 --> 43:57.439
Next episode! Who is Tamaya?

758
43:57.506 --> 44:00.309
Can he become this country's savior?

759
44:00.375 --> 44:02.044
Nipponia push!

760
44:02.111 --> 44:03.178
I'm absolutely against it!

761
44:03.245 --> 44:06.582
I don't see the point of issuing it, either.

762
44:06.648 --> 44:09.718
Will the Kokuji Party's power map change greatly?

763
44:09.785 --> 44:11.019
Seems like it will.

764
44:11.086 --> 44:12.888
But honestly, Tanuki don't care about that.

765
44:12.955 --> 44:16.492
The great thief will keep on stealing shady money, at her own pace.

766
44:17.159 --> 44:18.761
I'll spread the gold leaves!

767
44:19.828 --> 44:23.632
Then do politics so that you are not hated.

768
44:23.699 --> 44:26.668
Are you okay, Nipponia? Hang in there, Nipponia!

769
44:26.735 --> 44:28.604
I'm not okay at all!

770
44:28.670 --> 44:30.072
Subtitle translation by: Quang N.N.
